<title>USA: USDA allows import of 17 fruits, vegetables, herbs.</title>
<headline>USDA allows import of 17 fruits, vegetables, herbs.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-01-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>The U.S. Agriculture Department said Monday it would allow the importation of 17 previously barred fruits, vegetables and herbs from eight countries.</p>
<p>As a condition of entry, all will be subject to inspection or disinfection or both, USDA said. In addition, some of the fruits and vegetabales would be required to undergo treatment for pests or meet other conditions to mitigate the risk of alien pests becoming established in the United States.</p>
<p>Products to be allowed entry were:</p>
<p>--basil from Argentina.</p>
<p>--papaya from Belize.</p>
<p>--babaco from Colombia.</p>
<p>--angelica, an edible root, from South Korea.</p>
<p>--strawberries from Morocco.</p>
<p>--hyacinth and yard-long beans from Honduras.</p>
<p>--broad, green and mung beans from Nicaragua.</p>
<p>--clemintine, grapefruit, lemon, minneola, navel oranges, satsuma and valencia oranges from South Africa.</p>
</text>
